 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "in-plain-english",
   "heading": "In plain English",
   "role": "intuition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "in-plain-english-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "A partitioned matrix is a big matrix cut into blocks, each treated as a single entry. Block multiplication works like ordinary multiplication as long as the pieces fit and the order is kept. Splitting the regressors into a group of controls and a group of interest, and inverting in blocks, shows exactly how a coefficient's estimate and precision depend on the variation left in its variable after the controls are accounted for."
      },
      {
       "t": "callout",
       "kind": "why",
       "title": "Why it matters",
       "md": "It is the algebra behind controlling for variables, the Frisch-Waugh-Lovell theorem and omitted-variable bias.",
       "folded": false
      }
     ],
     "words": 89,
     "symbols": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "definition",
   "heading": "Definition",
   "role": "definition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "definition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Split a matrix into blocks and treat each block as an entry. Products work as with scalars — provided the block dimensions conform and you keep the order."
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "\\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\\\C&D\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}E\\\\F\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}AE + BF\\\\ CE + DF\\end{pmatrix}"
      }
     ],
     "words": 27,
     "symbols": [
      "$A, B, C, D$",
      "$E$, $F$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "regression-partition",
   "heading": "Regression partition",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "regression-partition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Split the regressors $\\mathbf{X} = [\\mathbf{X}_1\\ \\mathbf{X}_2]$. Then"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "\\mathbf{X}'\\mathbf{X} = \\begin{pmatrix}\\mathbf{X}_1'\\mathbf{X}_1 & \\mathbf{X}_1'\\mathbf{X}_2\\\\ \\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{X}_1 & \\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{X}_2\\end{pmatrix}"
      }
     ],
     "words": 4,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\mathbf{X}_1$, $\\mathbf{X}_2$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "the-partitioned-inverse",
   "heading": "The partitioned inverse",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "the-partitioned-inverse-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "For a symmetric block matrix with invertible $A$, the lower-right block of the inverse is the inverse of the **Schur complement** $D - CA^{-1}B$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "\\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\\\C&D\\end{pmatrix}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix}\\cdot & \\cdot\\\\ \\cdot & (D - CA^{-1}B)^{-1}\\end{pmatrix}"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Applied to $\\mathbf{X}'\\mathbf{X}$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "D - CA^{-1}B = \\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{X}_2 - \\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{X}_1(\\mathbf{X}_1'\\mathbf{X}_1)^{-1}\\mathbf{X}_1'\\mathbf{X}_2 = \\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{X}_2"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "where $\\mathbf{M}_1$ is the residual maker for $\\mathbf{X}_1$. So $[(\\mathbf{X}'\\mathbf{X})^{-1}]_{22} = (\\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{X}_2)^{-1}$."
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "**Numerical check** (20 observations, $\\mathbf{X}_1$ = constant and trend, $\\mathbf{X}_2$ random): the (3,3) element of $(\\mathbf{X}'\\mathbf{X})^{-1}$ and $1/(\\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{X}_2)$ agree to 15 digits."
      }
     ],
     "words": 47,
     "symbols": [
      "$A, B, C, D$",
      "$D - CA^{-1}B$",
      "$\\mathbf{X}_1$, $\\mathbf{X}_2$",
      "$\\mathbf{M}_1$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "why-it-matters",
   "heading": "Why it matters",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "why-it-matters-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Multiply out the block normal equations and solve for $\\mathbf{b}_2$: you get $\\mathbf{b}_2 = (\\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{X}_2)^{-1}\\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{y}$ — the [Frisch-Waugh-Lovell Theorem](note:frisch-waugh-lovell-theorem). The same block gives $\\mathrm{Var}(\\mathbf{b}_2) = \\sigma^2(\\mathbf{X}_2'\\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{X}_2)^{-1}$: a coefficient is precise only if its regressor has variation left **after** partialling out the others."
      }
     ],
     "words": 34,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\mathbf{X}_1$, $\\mathbf{X}_2$",
      "$\\mathbf{M}_1$",
      "$\\mathbf{b}_2$",
      "$\\sigma^2$",
      "$\\mathbf{y}$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
